Ritemark 2.0 surfaces and frame (Sprint 132, step 1)
The window in three levels, as drawn (ritemark-dev: product/design/experience, framework.md and
surfaces/README.md "Measurements"): a flat ground with no lines between the parts, the AI on its own
sheet 8 from the desk, the documents on a raised desk. This patch owns sizes and drawing; patch 002 keeps
what sits where; 019 owns the editor groups and the tab row.

The frame (R1, R2, R3): the main window has no title row, on the Mac and on Windows. Each column carries a
44 strip at its top (ritemarkFrameStrip.ts): the traffic lights (the Mac) or the application menu's button
(Windows, ritemarkFrameMenu.ts: VS Code's menu bar control showing only its overflow button) and the left
column's toggle over the left column; the tabs over the desk; the AI's toggle and You over the AI, then
Windows' own buttons. Everything in a strip sits on one line, 26 from the top. When a side column is
hidden, its controls move onto the desk's strip. The strips are drag regions and their controls are not.
The constants live in platform/window/common/window.ts, so the main process places the window controls
from the same numbers: the traffic lights centred in a 52 box, Windows' buttons 44 high on the ground's
colour (electron-browser titlebarPart.ts is their one sender; the hidden title bar no longer sends its own
height). Sheets (dialogs) hang from 44. The strips and the desk's tab row keep their height through zoom
(counter-zoomed in CSS, laid out at 44/zoom). Floating windows keep a title row, 44 high.

The AI column (R4): no title rows and no row of icons; a guard sends any other view container that lands
there back to its own home (Copilot Chat's container to the panel).
The status bar is 24 with the remote and problems signals hidden; the tree's rows are 32 (R1).
The left column's head on Documents (R10) and on Agents (experience 31; ritemarkProjectHead.ts): the project's mark
and name with a chevron, New and Search, and on Documents the Documents label over the tree; its menus come from
the extension (ritemark/projectHead, ritemark/projectHead/new and ritemark/projectHead/agents/new,
menusExtensionPoint.ts).
More (R5, R6): the places row's overflow opens Ritemark's menu as drawn: "More places", Copilot Chat first,
then the places that do not fit, with Ritemark's names (compositeBarActions.ts, ritemarkMenu.ts).
You (R11): Settings and Help, in the same menu. Ritemark's menus on every platform (R12): window.menuStyle
defaults to custom, and menu.ts draws them; the chosen row is the theme's menu selection (defaultStyles.ts).
Dialogs (experience 31): window.dialogStyle defaults to custom too, so a dialog is Ritemark's own sheet, drawn in
ritemarkSurfaces.css (400 wide, the title, the detail, Don't ask again beside the buttons, the default button ringed).
The palette and notifications (the Experience lead's step-1 spec): the quick input on the menus' sheet with radius 10,
a 32 input and 32 rows (quickInputList.ts and the tree's delegate give the rows their 32); toasts and the centre on the
menus' sheet with the message 13/20 (notificationsViewer.ts measures with that line) and 32-high buttons.
A new window's columns are the drawn widths: the left column 256 (248 and the 8 to the desk), the AI column
400 (the 8, the AI's sheet 340 and its rail 52), so the desk is 784 at 1440 (layout.ts).
Window sizes (R14, ritemarkWindowSizes.ts, loaded by desktop.contribution.ts): below 1400 the AI's column narrows
to 336 (its sheet 320), below 1200 the left column hides, below 960 the AI's column hides; crossing back up undoes
only what the crossing down did, and a column the person opens in between stays until the next crossing.
The stylesheet (media/ritemarkSurfaces.css) takes every colour from the theme; each !important says on its line which
rule of VS Code's (or inline style, or of its own) it beats, and where none competes in any state there is none.
Also: a click on the open place in the top row runs ritemark.placeClickedAgain, which the extension answers
(Tasks brings its board forward); with a flow open, Ritemark's own .ritemark folder stays out of the tree.
Built file by file from the Experience lead's proof of concept (branch poc/2.0-surfaces), without its
proof-of-concept-only code.

Sprint 133 (the welcome): while the extension's welcome is the editor in front and it says so (the context key
ritemark.welcomeAlone), ritemarkWelcomeAlone.ts puts ritemark-welcome-alone on the workbench: only the welcome's sheet
on the ground, with the strips' controls, the tab row, the desk's paint and the status bar hidden but nothing written;
Windows' menu button is not mounted and the window sizes stand down meanwhile (R1). An empty desk's tiles are drawn
here (019 makes them). A file's extension is drawn apart from its name and muted, in the tree and on tabs: the
resource label's splitExtension option (base/browser/ui/iconLabel/iconLabel.ts, workbench/browser/labels.ts), asked
for by the Explorer's rows (explorerViewer.ts) and the tabs (019) (R13).

Sprint 134 (the project at the centre): the Task board tab's count is drawn here, at the icon's lower right as the
places row draws its badge (019 makes it; R6). The head (ritemarkProjectHead.ts) gains its rows on Home (no title row),
Tasks (New task, Search), Browser and Transcribe (Record, Transcribe a file…), each row a command the extension
contributes (R16, R17); the project menu's Switch project opens a flyout of the projects the extension lists
(ritemark.projects.switcherItems; ritemarkMenu.ts gains submenus, a checked mark and Alt-click). File ▸ New tab,
Switch project and Share come from the extension's ritemark/fileMenu (menusExtensionPoint.ts; R8, R21). The project
root's AGENTS.md is named About this project in the tree and on tabs, with its tooltip and accessible name (labels.ts,
explorerViewer.ts), and Documents hides the root CLAUDE.md while it is only Ritemark's pointer line `@AGENTS.md`
(FilesFilter reads it, as it reads .gitignore; R8). A switch that reloads the window (ShutdownReason.LOAD: Open Recent,
Open Folder…, a drop, Close Folder) waits for the extension's _ritemark.projects.beforeReload, passed the folder the
host service noted (ritemarkSwitchGuard.ts, nativeHostService.ts); with no command, or no answer and no question shown
in 10 s, the reload goes on; Reload Window and quitting never ask (R9). The splash saves ritemark.ground as the
window's background, so a switch shows the ground, not the desk (partsSplash.ts; the switch audit). A modal message
whose extension lists its close affordance first gets Cancel as its default, focused and drawn primary
(mainThreadMessageService.ts, dialogHandler.ts, dialogs.ts' ICustomDialogOptions.ritemarkFocusCancel, dialog.ts): the
switch question with no known folder, where the other button stops the AI (S33). The dialog's button row starts at the
sheet's edge and wraps rather than running past it (three buttons, S31). A menu row is read with what it shows at its
right ("1 waits", "Not found", a key); a long name ends in … before it; a project whose folder is not found is muted, with
its old place under its name and a warning at its right (S38). The project's mark is two letters, as the welcome draws it
("Notes" → "NO"). The project menu's New folder and Collapse folders show on Documents only.

Sprint 135: rebased onto VS Code 1.142.0 (the sprint's research/frame-audit.md). Modern UI
(workbench.experimental.modernUI) is pinned off in product.json; the frame's lines sit beside its branches.
sidebarPart.ts and auxiliaryBarPart.ts answer upstream's getRelayoutDimension() with the full size, so a strip is
not taken off twice; windowImpl.ts places the window controls inside upstream's hasWindowControlOverlay gate;
overlayWebview.ts sets ritemark-desk-webview in setAnchorElement (webviews now follow their anchor by CSS anchor
positioning); ritemarkSurfaces.css styles the empty desk through .editor-group-watermark-wrapper.
Depends on 007: the hook in paneCompositeBar.ts uses the ICommandService that 007 injects into
ViewContainerActivityAction (no shared lines, so the stacking check cannot see it; regenerate 018 if 007 drops it).

Sprint 140 (2.1, R11–R13; experience 34, frames 2, 3 and 5): the feedback sheet over the whole window
(ritemarkFeedbackSheet.ts and media/ritemarkFeedbackSheet.css, loaded by desktop.contribution.ts), 440 wide over the
dimmed window. The extension opens it with _ritemark.feedbackSheet and gives every word; the sheet's route words follow
its two boxes live (the AI box puts the last answer's box in place of "I'd like an answer", as frame 3 draws it), Send
is off until something is typed, Enter makes a new line, Cmd+Enter (Ctrl+Enter) presses the button, Escape closes it,
Tab stays inside. Its button asks the extension's _ritemark.feedback.submit; the sheet closes when the message was
accepted, keeps the message with one line, Try again and Open in e-mail when it did not go (D5), and becomes frame 5's
hand-off, with Copy and Done, when an e-mail app was asked to open. It resolves with sent, mailed or cancelled.

Sprint 142 (2.1 polish, merge 2): File changes is Source Control's name (#557, experience 3): the SCM view container's
title and its views' container title (contrib/scm/browser/scm.contribution.ts), so More, the panel's head and View ▸
Advanced (002) read it; More's own name for it goes (compositeBarActions.ts). The Repositories and Graph views and the
Settings group keep upstream's Source Control for now (#567). The desk's edge
is whole (#503; framework §4; the sprint's research/sheet-edges-audit.md, A6): the desk sits flush with the boxes that
clip it, so it has no shadow and draws its hairline inside its own box with ::after, over a webview's layer too
(.ritemark-desk-webview); the welcome alone draws none. ritemark.sheetShadow stays for what floats (menus, dialogs,
quick input, toasts), not the desk or the AI's sheet.

Sprint 144 (2.1.0's Gate 1, #503's follow-up): the chosen tab joins the desk again with no line beneath it, as in 2.0
(framework §1, "joined to it like a browser tab"; Jarmo's Gate 1 finding on the release candidate). Sprint 142's
hairline lies inside the desk's box, where the tab row (clipped at the desk's top, and under the hairline's layer) can't
cover it. So the desk lays its own colour over its top edge under the chosen tab and its two fillets
(.editor-container::before): a fillet and its hairline past each side (--rm-fillet-box, from --rm-fillet and
--rm-hairline-width, which the fillets and the hairlines now read too) and 1.5 hairlines high, at z-index 11, over the
hairline (10) and a desk webview's layer. It is fixed and placed by anchor positioning: the chosen tab (anchor-name on
.tab.active) gives its ends, and the tab row's own box (its scrolling box, 8 in from the desk's edge at least) is its
limit, the Experience lead's rule (experience 3): it never cuts a corner, nothing lands on the ground, it stops where a
fillet cut at the row's end stops, and beside Home the corner's curve rises straight into the fillet in one S, as the
kit's single outline and 2.0 draw it. Its default anchor is the title row, which never scrolls (with the tab as the
default, a scrolled tab row in a very narrow desk shifted the bound too); anchor-scope keeps each desk to its own. The
other edges, the top edge beside the tab, no shadow and the welcome alone stay as Sprint 142 drew them.

2.1.0's Gate 1 join pass (RC3; the Experience lead's rulings at 04eba74 and 0d0c1b8: framework §§1 and 4, the kit,
experience 3): the desk's edge is drawn once, and opaque. The hairline lies over a line of the desk's own colour
(--rm-desk-edge), so it reads one tone over a webview's page, a terminal or the browser's page: #302d56 in Ritemark
Dark, where the #FFFFFF14 hairline over #1E1B4B was drawn twice under a webview and read #403d63. Where a webview lies
on the desk (.editor-container:has() the webview editor's element), its layer draws the edge and the desk's own line
under it goes. The chosen tab's outline (--rm-tab-edge) and its fillets lie over the same desk-coloured line, so the
desk and its tab are one tone; the AI's sheet keeps its own (#2b294d). What floats keeps 4 clear of any line it runs
beside or lies across, and a menu that lands on a control covers it whole, corners included, or keeps 4 clear of it:
the + menu and the folded tabs' menu open 4 below the desk's top line (their edge at 49 for the line at 44) and end 8
in from the desk's right edge (019 places them from the desk), so they cover the document bar's Share whole; their width
is a whole pixel (calc-size), so the edge is crisp. The folded tabs' button ("N ⌄") is a whole pixel wide the same way,
so its left edge and its hover and open fill land on whole device pixels. You's menu ends 4 past You's right edge,
covering the AI rail's + and history whole. The left column's slider stops 2 CSS pixels short of the desk's left
hairline at every scale. And the left column lays its content out again when the head's rows change after its first
draw (a window that opens on Documents drew its head before the extension's New and Search existed, and the tree ran
64 past the column's foot, under the status bar): ritemarkProjectHead.ts fires onDidChangeRows, sidebarPart.ts lays
out again.

Sprint 145 (#569): a window that a switch in place opened lands on Home from its first frame. When the switch guard lets a
LOAD go on and no other participant vetoes it, it writes a note into application storage with the storage's last save
(onWillSaveState, SHUTDOWN): the folder left, the folder opened when the window knew it, the time. The next main editor
part takes it once as it makes its desks, before the grid is made, and puts Home's tab first in its desk's order of
use and that desk in front, so Home is the tab in front and the document the project was left on stays behind it
(ritemarkSwitchGuard.ts: ritemarkLandsOnHome, the extension's own rule; ritemarkHomeInFront; editorPart.ts). Until now
the restored document was in front for about half a second, until the extension started and brought Home forward.
While a column waits for its place or the AI at start, it shows the ground, not VS Code's "Drag a view here to
display." (ritemarkSurfaces.css), which flashed in the left column at every start and, with 002's AI column restored
with the layout, would have in the AI column too. The held start, as the Experience lead set it: the AI column shows no
progress bar (an indigo line, which framework §3 rules out), nothing for its first second, and then, while its view still
waits for the extension, the kit's turning mark (ink on the strong hairline, 16) at its centre, alone, labelled "The AI is
starting" for screen readers; it goes when the view is resolved (contrib/webviewView/browser/webviewViewPane.ts, only for a
view in the AI column; ritemarkSurfaces.css). And the left column lays out once: until the extensions are read, a head
row whose command is not registered yet keeps its room, empty (`.ritemark-project-row-waiting`), so New and Search fill
rows already drawn and the tree under them never moves (on main a window landing on Documents drew the tree, then pushed
it down 64 when New and Search arrived); once the extensions are read, a row whose command is still missing goes
(ritemarkProjectHead.ts).

